WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.065 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two measuring displays disagree about who has
the bigger amount.

00:00:04.185 --> 00:00:08.285 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One display has more digits, but does that make
its amount greater?

00:00:08.405 --> 00:00:12.832 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Let’s use matching places and a zoomable number
line to solve the mystery.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.077 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, we’re learning to compare, order and
locate decimals through thousandths.

00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:23.955 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to line up places and use end
zeros without changing a decimal’s value.

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:28.548 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to compare and order decimals
using place value.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.870 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You’ll be able to explain where a decimal
belongs between two number-line marks.

00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:37.590 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Does a longer decimal always mean a bigger
number?

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:38.520 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
No.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.915 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Compare place values instead.

00:00:41.035 --> 00:00:45.754 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Start with the whole number parts, then tenths,
hundredths and thousandths.

00:00:45.874 --> 00:00:51.781 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Adding zeros at the end of a decimal can help
line up the places without changing its value.

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.897 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which display shows the greater amount?

00:00:55.017 --> 00:00:59.832 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Try matching the decimal places, rather than
counting how many digits you see.

00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:05.611 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Compare one point four zero seven and one point
four seven.

00:01:05.731 --> 00:01:09.090 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Write the second as one point four seven zero.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.986 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Ones and tenths match.

00:01:11.106 --> 00:01:14.276 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
At the hundredths place, zero is less than
seven.

00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:19.458 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
So one point four zero seven is less than one
point four seven zero.

00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:27.043 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
For ordering, line up two point zero five three,
two point three zero five, and two point three
five zero.

00:01:27.163 --> 00:01:31.161 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The first is smallest because its tenths digit
is zero.

00:01:31.281 --> 00:01:36.281 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The other two match in tenths, but zero
hundredths is less than five hundredths.

00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:39.511 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Where might one point four three seven lie?

00:01:39.631 --> 00:01:42.177 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
First find its neighbouring hundredths.

00:01:42.297 --> 00:01:44.520 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Then we can zoom in together.

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.452 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now locate a number on a line.

00:01:48.572 --> 00:01:54.845 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
From one point four zero to one point five zero,
divide the interval into ten equal steps.

00:01:54.965 --> 00:01:57.107 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Each step is one hundredth.

00:01:57.227 --> 00:02:02.332 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One point four three and one point four four are
neighbouring marks on this line.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:10.238 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
To locate one point four three seven more
precisely, zoom in between one point four three
and one point four four.

00:02:10.358 --> 00:02:13.787 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Divide that interval into ten equal steps.

00:02:13.907 --> 00:02:16.192 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Each step is one thousandth.

00:02:16.312 --> 00:02:22.021 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Count seven steps from one point four three zero
to reach one point four three seven.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.940 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
A longer decimal is not automatically larger.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.785 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One point four zero seven is smaller than one
point four seven.

00:02:30.905 --> 00:02:37.382 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Adding an end zero to make one point four seven
zero changes its name, but not its value.

00:02:37.733 --> 00:02:42.229 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Which is greater: 2.405 or 2.45?

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:51.011 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Write 2.45 as 2.450.

00:02:51.131 --> 00:02:55.435 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
At the hundredths place, five hundredths is
greater than zero hundredths.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:03.339 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Finally, four point seven equals four point
seven zero zero: both represent seven tenths
after four wholes.

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:06.521 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Extra end zeros do not add any value.

00:03:06.641 --> 00:03:13.615 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Remember: line up the places, compare from the
left, and check the equal step size before
locating a decimal.

00:03:13.967 --> 00:03:18.523 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Our goal was to compare, order and locate
decimals through thousandths.

00:03:18.643 --> 00:03:24.430 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You can now explain your strategy, check the
places carefully, and show why your answer makes
sense.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.171 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Choose a number of your own and explain it to
someone nearby.
